# Test Instructions

## Structure & File Conventions

- Tests live in `tests/` mirroring `src/{{ pkg_name }}/` (e.g. `src/{{ pkg_name }}/database.py` → `tests/test_database.py`).
- Group related tests inside a class named `Test<Subject>`.
- Each test file imports only from public module APIs — never reach into private helpers unless explicitly testing them.

## Fixtures & Helpers

- Always use `tmp_path` for filesystem operations — never hardcode temp paths.
{% if "pytest" in testing_frameworks %}
- Use pytest fixtures for shared setup — prefer `conftest.py` for fixtures shared across multiple test files.
{% endif %}

## Mocking Rules

- Mock at the boundary of the unit under test — never mock internals of the module you are testing.
{% if "mock" in testing_frameworks %}
- Use `unittest.mock.patch` as a context manager or decorator — prefer `patch.object` over string-path mocking when the target is importable directly.
- Use `MagicMock` for objects with dynamic attribute access; use `Mock` for simple callables.
{% endif %}
{% if db_driver != "none" %}
- In database tests, mock at the session/connection level — do not use a real database in unit tests. Use a temporary SQLite `:memory:` database for integration tests only.
{% endif %}

## Test Quality Standards

- Test names must describe the scenario: `test_<function>_<condition>_<expected_outcome>`.
- Assert one logical concept per test; split unrelated assertions into separate tests.
- Cover error paths and edge cases — not just the happy path.
{% if "pytest" in testing_frameworks %}

## pytest Conventions

- Use `pytest.raises(ExceptionType)` for expected exceptions — never try/except in tests.
- Use parametrize for table-driven tests: `@pytest.mark.parametrize("input,expected", [...])`.
- Mark slow tests with `@pytest.mark.slow` so they can be excluded during fast local runs.
{% endif %}
{% if "unittest" in testing_frameworks %}

## unittest Conventions

- Extend `unittest.TestCase`; use `setUp` / `tearDown` for shared state.
- Use `self.assert*` methods — do not mix pytest assertions with `unittest.TestCase`.
{% endif %}

## When to Add Tests

Always add tests when:
- Adding a new public function or method.
- Fixing a bug — add a test that would have caught it.
- Changing conditional logic (new branch = new test).
- Adding a new CLI command or argument.
